✅ QUESTION 4: SMALLEST WINDOW IN A STRING CONTAINING ALL DISTINCT CHARACTERS

This one is pure sliding window mastery and shows you understand optimization + frequency tracking.

🔹 Problem Statement

You are given a string s.

Your task is to find the smallest substring (window) of s that contains all distinct characters present in the entire string.

✏️ Example
Input:  s = "aabcbcdbca"
Output: "dbca"

Explanation

Distinct characters in s are:

{a, b, c, d}


Smallest window containing all → "dbca"

🔹 Brute Force (for understanding ❌)

Check all substrings

Time Complexity: O(n³) → not acceptable

🔹 Optimized Approach: Sliding Window + HashMap
💡 Key Insight

First, count total distinct characters in the string

Expand window until all are included

Shrink window to minimize length

🔹 Algorithm (Step-by-Step)
Step 1:

Count number of distinct characters in s

Step 2:

Use two pointers:

start → beginning of window

end → expanding pointer

Step 3:

When window contains all distinct characters:

Try shrinking from left to find minimum window

⏱ Time Complexity: O(n)
📦 Space Complexity: O(1) (at most 256 characters)

🔹 Java Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public String smallestWindow(String s) {
        int n = s.length();

        // Count total distinct characters
        Set<Character> distinctSet = new HashSet<>();
        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {
            distinctSet.add(ch);
        }
        int required = distinctSet.size();

        Map<Character, Integer> windowMap = new HashMap<>();
        int start = 0, startIndex = -1;
        int minLen = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int count = 0;

        for (int end = 0; end < n; end++) {
            char ch = s.charAt(end);
            windowMap.put(ch, windowMap.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);

            if (windowMap.get(ch) == 1) {
                count++;
            }

            // Try to minimize window
            while (count == required) {
                if (end - start + 1 < minLen) {
                    minLen = end - start + 1;
                    startIndex = start;
                }

                char leftChar = s.charAt(start);
                windowMap.put(leftChar, windowMap.get(leftChar) - 1);

                if (windowMap.get(leftChar) == 0) {
                    count--;
                }
                start++;
            }
        }

        if (startIndex == -1) return "";
        return s.substring(startIndex, startIndex + minLen);
    }
}

🔹 Line-by-Line Explanation
Set<Character> distinctSet = new HashSet<>();


Finds total unique characters in the string.

windowMap.put(ch, windowMap.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);


Tracks character frequency inside current window.

if (windowMap.get(ch) == 1)


New character added → increase count.

while (count == required)


Window contains all distinct characters — now try shrinking.

windowMap.put(leftChar, windowMap.get(leftChar) - 1);


Remove character from left side of window.

